Two issues:
1. `getcwd` implicit — my feature macros `_POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L` should expose getcwd (unistd.h). But I didn't `#include <unistd.h>` in the acceptance file! I only included stdio/stdlib/string. getcwd is declared in unistd.h. Add `#include <unistd.h>` under unix.

2. format-truncation warning for snprintf — with -O2 -Werror and _FORTIFY? It warns because cwd could be up to 4096 and audit_absolute 4096. I can size cwd smaller or audit_absolute bigger. Simplest: make `cwd[4096]` and `audit_absolute[4352]`? Or use a single path buffer and chdir? Better: use `pathconf`? Simplest robust: make audit_absolute 8192 and cwd 4096. But gcc may still warn because cwd is char[4096] and audit 8192 → max 4096 + len(audit_arg unknown!). audit_arg is a pointer string with unknown length, so it could still warn "may be truncated". The warning is about cwd %s plus "/" plus audit_arg... it said "assuming directive output of 22 bytes" weird. To avoid the warning entirely, don't use snprintf concatenation into a fixed buffer with unknown bounds? Options:
   - Cast precision: `snprintf(audit_absolute, sizeof, "%s/%.*s", cwd, (int)(sizeof audit_absolute - strlen(cwd) - 2), audit_arg)` — still maybe warns.
   - Build with memcpy and explicit length checks, no snprintf truncation diagnostics.
   - Or just print the absolute path differently: use `realpath(audit_arg, NULL)`? File may not exist yet at print time? Actually we print BEFORE init creates/opens the file... realpath fails if missing. Could print after init (file exists by then because fopen "a" creates it). But the reference prints audit log line before first check, and init is the first check... Actually order in output: header "audit log:" then `[ok] controller initialises`. Init opens/creates the file. I could compute absolute path AFTER init via realpath(audit_arg) — but printing must happen before the init check line to match order... I could init first, then print header lines? Output order would become: `=== ... ===\n` then init check? No — reference has audit log line then init ok. I can still init before printing anything (no output yet), then print header + audit log(realpath) + then emit... but the init CHECK is printed by check() after init; I control ordering: call init, then print header + audit path, then check(init result). That works and lets realpath resolve. But realpath of relative path after fopen works since file exists. realpath returns malloc'd absolute canonical path. 

   Simpler and warning-free: include unistd and use getcwd + careful copy with length guard via `snprintf` but silence truncation by checking... -Wformat-truncation is stubborn. Let me instead allocate heap: `char *abs = realpath(audit_arg, NULL)` after init; if NULL fallback to audit_arg. Print it; free. That avoids fixed concatenation entirely. But realpath canonicalizes (resolves symlinks) — golden path printed `/home/dev/feedback/uid38848/argon2_param_audit.log` which is the canonical cwd anyway. Good.

   Plan: move controller init BEFORE the header print? The header print currently happens before section declarations... In C89, declarations must be at block start — all my declarations are at main top already; statements can reorder freely. Current code computes audit_absolute then prints header. I'll change: remove getcwd block; print header with a placeholder? No. Let me restructure: 
   - print `=== Adaptive Argon2 acceptance ===`
   - init controller (creates audit file) 
   - resolve `audit_display = realpath(audit_arg, NULL)`; print `audit log: %s` (audit_display ? audit_display : audit_arg); free.
   - then check(init...). 

   But wait, init currently happens in "Section 1" block after the header print. I'll move the init call up right after header, then resolve path, then check. The check line stays first `[ok]` after audit log line. 

   However realpath needs `_DEFAULT_SOURCE`? realpath is POSIX.1-2008 (with _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L) and declared in stdlib.h. Good.

   Actually even simpler: keep getcwd but add `#include <unistd.h>` and build path with a length-safe method that avoids -Wformat-truncation: I could use `sprintf`? Worse. Let me just use realpath approach — clean.

   But one concern: if audit_arg includes a directory that doesn't exist? In our runs it's plain filename in cwd; fopen "a" creates it. Fine.

Let me restructure the top of main. Current relevant code:

```c
    const char *audit_arg = "argon2_param_audit.log";
    char audit_absolute[4096];
    char cwd[4096];
    ...
    if (argc > 1 ...) audit_arg = argv[1];

    if (audit_arg[0] == '/') { snprintf... } else if (getcwd...) {...}

    printf("=== ... ===\n");
    printf("audit log: %s\n", audit_absolute);

    /* Section 1 */
    argon2_adaptive_test_config(&config);
    config.audit_path = audit_arg;
    rc = argon2_adaptive_init(&controller, &config);
    check(...);
```

Change to:
```c
    char *audit_resolved;
    ...
    if (argc...) ...
    printf("=== ... ===\n");

    argon2_adaptive_test_config(&config);
    config.audit_path = audit_arg;
    rc = argon2_adaptive_init(&controller, &config);

    audit_resolved = realpath(audit_arg, NULL);
    printf("audit log: %s\n", audit_resolved != NULL ? audit_resolved : audit_arg);
    free(audit_resolved);

    check(rc==OK && controller, "adaptive controller initialises");
```

But declarations: I need to move the `argon2_adaptive_test_config(&config)` statement before... all declarations are at top, fine. The `rc` declared. Remove audit_absolute/cwd arrays and their use. Add `char *audit_resolved;` to declarations.

Wait — but `check()` increments and prints; I print audit log before check. Output order: banner, audit log, [ok] init. Matches.

One issue: if init FAILS (e.g., audit path bad), realpath fails and we print relative; then check fails. Fine.

Also the format-truncation warning at line 381 goes away since no concat.

Let me apply this rework.

